{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And He made them houses.</b> For these two midwives, who are Yocheved and Miriam her daughter: Yocheved is called Shiphrah because she makes (משפרת) the child beautiful, and Miriam is called Puah because she vocalizes (פועה) at the child." "<b>And the verse, by way of the plain sense (peshat):</b> 'And it came to pass, because the midwives feared Elohim' — more than the fear of the king — 'that He made them houses' — for Israel, that is, that He set for them guards and stationed one Egyptian house between every two Israelite houses, so that they would not be able to hide the children from them. And likewise (Exodus 15:21), ״ותען להם מרים״ refers back to Israel, not to the women who went out after her. And some interpreted 'houses' as prison houses, that they would be shut up there forever." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> 'And He made them houses' — He blessed them with ancestral houses: houses of priesthood and Levites from Yocheved, and royalty from Miriam. And thus it is written (Psalms 135:19-20): ״בית אהרן ברכו את ה' בית הלוי ברכו את ה'״. And according to this Midrash, the word 'and He made' does not refer back to Pharaoh but to Elohim. And the fact that it says 'And Pharaoh charged' and not 'and he charged all his people' proves this, that the word 'and He made' refers back to HaShem, blessed be He; therefore He needed to mention the name of Pharaoh. And the word 'for them (להם)' returns to the midwives, and it should have said להן, for such is the rule of the language for females — and likewise above it says ״ויאמר להן מדוע עשיתן״. But we find that this is the way of Scripture in various places, for thus we find (Exodus 2:17): ״ויבאו הרועים ויגרשום״, and afterward it says ״ויושיען וישק את צאנם״ —" "<b>And perhaps</b> the reason is this: that Scripture gives these midwives the grammatical status of males as reward for the commandment in which they engage — to keep the males alive. Therefore it uses of them two forms, both the feminine and the masculine: once להם, once להן. And further, because the tribe of Levi embraces both priesthood and Levitical service, which correspond to Mercy and Judgment — and understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:12.252809039Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}